engagement and retention.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:03.560
Let's start with the development
phase category of products.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:06.280
Let's take a look at your normal
app architecture, something

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:07.870
that most of you
probably recognize

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:09.580
from your own projects.

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:11.110
You've got your
mobile app, which

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:14.080
communicates with your main
frontend server using a REST

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:15.070
API.

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:18.730
That server talks to your
all other backend services.

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:21.310
You've probably got a
server for authentication

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:23.290
and a server for media files.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:26.260
You also have a database, which
is one of the first decisions

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:28.150
that app developers make.

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:30.550
You may be concerned
about your infrastructure

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:32.860
and how it will grow
with your company.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:35.650
What about if you want
to include new platforms?

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:37.900
How would you scale
your frontend server?

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:40.210
Is your authentication
server secure?

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:42.100
Is your media server
terabytes ready,

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:44.440
and database fast enough?

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.210
Instead of the
diagram before, here

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:48.670
is what it looks
like with Firebase.

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:50.590
Let's build together
a mobile app that

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:53.090
process user-generated content.

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:54.880
It could be a photo
album, accessible

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.280
from both mobile
and web interfaces,

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.120
that attacks and extracts from
any image uploaded by the user

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.800
and presents the result
back to the user.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:06.430
The first step in designing
our app architecture

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:08.440
is user authentication.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:12.190
Firebase authentication is
an out-of-box authentication

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:16.750
UI that supports email and
password phone authentication,

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:19.490
and Facebook and many others.

00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:21.730
Now that the user
is authenticated,

00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:25.210
they can upload their image to
Cloud storage, a secure place

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:27.370
in the Cloud for user content.

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:30.910
The event of a new image
uploaded to Cloud Storage

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:33.940
will trigger Cloud
Functions that, in our case,

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:37.510
will perform optical character
recognition on the image.

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:40.390
Firebase ML Kit makes it
easy to access machine

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:42.320
learning models from your app.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:45.100
Now that we have extracted
the text from the image,

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:48.400
we will store it in Cloud
Firestore, a real time NoSQL

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:49.420
database.

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:53.170
A Cloud app can subscribe to
notifications of new data being

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:55.060
stored in Cloud Firestore.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:57.880
And so our users quickly
receive a notification

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:01.120
of what text has been
extracted from their image.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:04.960
The user confirms that the
text is indeed correct.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:07.450
If we were building a
Cloud-native app from scratch,

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:09.880
we may finish our work here.

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:12.340
But what if we're adding
a Cloud-native module

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:15.610
to an existing legacy app that's
running most of its legacy

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:17.050
logic on-prem?

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:20.290
In that case, you can
simply expose the Secure API

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:23.860
to your legacy backend and
utilize Cloud Functions

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:26.860
to notify the backend
every time user approved

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:29.140
a new entry to Cloud Firestore.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:31.330
Now that we covered the
benefits of developing

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:34.540
apps with Firebase, let's talk
about keeping them healthy.

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:36.700
Firebase can help here too.

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:38.450
So you have built your app.

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:40.910
It's got a huge list
of great features.

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:42.730
It's doing well in
your alpha test.

00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:45.830
You're sure it's better
than anything out there.

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:48.310
But what if your application
is buggy or unstable

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:49.790
in the real world?

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:51.910
You may receive bad reviews.

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:55.240
If your application crashes,
user may become unhappy

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:57.010
and might leave
negative reviews that

00:05:57.010 --> 00:05:59.410
scare away potential new users.

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:00.820
What can you do?

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:03.680
Let's see how Firebase
can help you too.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:06.010
You have just finished
developing a new feature

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:09.160
for your application and you're
ready to publish the updated

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:11.380
application to Apple
and Play Stores.

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:13.150
When rolling out
a new feature, you

00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:16.510
may want to push it to a small
percentage of users first.

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:19.570
With Firebase, you are in
full control of your rollout.

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:22.420
First, before you upload a
new build to the app stores,

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:24.430
you can put a new feature
behind the feature

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:26.500
flag using Remote Config.

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:29.290
Then, using Google
Analytics for Firebase,

00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:30.880
you can select
the one percentage

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:33.910
of users who will experience
the new feature first.

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:38.170
Once the one percentage of users
have the new feature enabled,

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:42.160
you can use Firebase Performance
Monitoring and crash analytics

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:45.790
to monitor user experience
who have new feature enabled

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:48.190
and look for any
potential app crashes

00:06:48.190 --> 00:06:50.080
that new feature may cause.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:52.000
We can dive more
deeply into our data

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:53.680
by exporting it to BigQuery.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:56.590
Soon, we'll have identified
that the app only crashes

00:06:56.590 --> 00:06:58.630
for the users with
very old devices,

00:06:58.630 --> 00:07:01.570
but works fine on almost
100% of the devices.

00:07:01.570 --> 00:07:04.390
At this point, you may want to
exclude the problematic devices

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:07.750
from your rollout while allowing
the vast majority of your users

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:09.620
to enjoy the new feature.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:13.300
Finally, we have arrived at
the perhaps most critical stage

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:15.430
of the application lifecycle.

00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:18.620
Once the application built with
Firebase is out in the world

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:22.600
and you know it performs as
expected with all Firebase

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:24.970
monitoring dashboards
indicating health,

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:27.220
you can focus on
acquiring and retaining

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:29.200
your most valuable users.

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:32.110
First and foremost, you
need to know your users.

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:35.440
With Google Analytics, you
can easily divide your users

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.870
into audience segments
so you can further

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:40.060
personalize their experience.

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:42.610
Next, with Predictions,
you can utilize

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:46.030
machine learning to predict
future user behavior based

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:48.980
on the historical events
captured in Analytics.

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:52.180
Finally, if you really need
to dive deeply into your data,

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:55.030
you can mirror all the
events to BigQuery.

00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:58.570
After you understand and start
segmenting your user base,

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:01.960
it's time to start experimenting
optimizing your application

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:03.190
performance.

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:05.830
Core to this is
Remote Config, that

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:08.590
allows you to remotely change
your application behavior

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:10.720
without the need to
ship a new build,

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:14.410
which would be time consuming
and inconvenient for the user.

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:17.560
Combined with Google
Analytics and A/B testing,

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:20.320
you can design and
execute experiments

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:23.770
to test what makes the
most optimal app experience

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:26.030
for a targeted segment.

00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:29.680
Finally, you need tools to
help retain your existing users

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:31.120
and acquire new ones.

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:34.120
In-app messaging offers
push notifications

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:36.100
that pop up in
the app to present

00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:39.640
your most valuable users with
a special offer, for example.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:41.650
Cloud Messaging
will help you reach

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:44.650
your users who haven't
visited your app in a few days

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:45.910
to reengage them.

00:08:45.910 --> 00:08:48.460
Finally, if you're planning
a marketing campaign

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:50.110
and you want to
advertise your app

00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:52.990
on, say, third-party
websites, Dynamic Links

00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:54.300
will be a great tool.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:56.560
Dynamic Links will help
your prospects look

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:59.140
at your app in the
respective app stores.

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:02.260
If the user clicks on the
link from an Android device,

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:04.930
they will automatically
land in the Play Store,

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:09.280
while an iOS user will be
pointed to the Apple store.

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:11.950
Many customers use Firebase
to build and operate

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:13.220
their applications.

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:15.670
One of them is
Surfboard Payments,

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:17.830
a Swedish-based
fintech developing

00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:20.380
next-generation
payment solutions.

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:22.300
By downloading
their application,

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:26.080
any merchant can start accepting
electronic payments in minutes.

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:28.840
Surfboard Payments
used Firebase in order

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:32.320
to be able to prototype
quickly, spot errors

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:35.080
by testing before
releasing, and sync

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:38.290
in-store user-generated
content in real time.

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:40.390
Now, let's go over a
short demonstration

00:09:40.390 --> 00:09:43.450
of how to connect an
Android app to Firebase.

00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:46.510
Step one, create a
Firebase project.

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:49.840
In the Firebase console,
click add project.

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:53.980
To create a new project, enter
the desired project name.

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:57.430
If prompted, review and
accept the Firebase terms.

00:09:57.430 --> 00:09:59.020
Click Continue.

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:01.420
Optional step is to
set up Google Analytics

00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:03.910
for your project or use
an existing account.

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:06.460
And I'm going to use my
Analytics account, which I

00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:08.470
already created for this demo.

00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:10.420
Click Create Project.

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:12.700
Firebase automatically
provisions resources

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:15.220
for your Firebase project.

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:17.110
When the process
is complete, you'll

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:20.290
be taken to the Overview page
for your Firebase project

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:22.000
in the Firebase console.

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:25.750
Step two, register your
application with Firebase.

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:27.580
Go to the Firebase console.

00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:30.070
In the center of the
Project Overview page,

00:10:30.070 --> 00:10:32.830
click the Android
icon or Add App

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:34.360
to launch the setup workflow.

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:38.965
Enter your app's package name in
the Android package name field.

00:10:38.965 --> 00:10:40.810
Click Register App.

00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:44.020
Step three, add Firebase
configuration file.

00:10:44.020 --> 00:10:46.780
To add the Firebase
Android configuration file,

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:50.650
first click Download
google-services.json file

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:53.770
to obtain your Firebase
Android configuration file.

00:10:53.770 --> 00:10:56.680
In the second step, move
your configuration file

00:10:56.680 --> 00:11:00.730
into the module app-level
directory of your app.

00:11:00.730 --> 00:11:02.710
In the project-level
Gradle file,

00:11:02.710 --> 00:11:06.040
add rules to include Google
services Gradle plugin.

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:09.190
Make sure that you have Google's
Maven repository as well.

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:15.220
In the app-level Gradle file,
apply the Google services

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:15.895
Gradle plugin.

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:22.170
Step four, add Firebase SDK.

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:25.800
Using the Firebase Android
BoM, declare the dependencies

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:29.400
for your Firebase products that
you want to use in your app.

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:32.700
Declare them in your
app-level Gradle file.

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:36.030
Sync your app in order to ensure
that all dependencies have

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:38.460
the necessary versions.

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:39.390
And that's it.

00:11:39.390 --> 00:11:42.690
You managed to successfully
add your app to Firebase.

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:45.420
And this brings us to the
end of this video, where

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:48.120
we learned that Firebase
has an array of offerings

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:51.270
that help developers to
work better and faster.

00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:53.100
To continue to
explore what Firebase

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:56.100
has to offer in further details,
check out our suggestions

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:56.910
here--

00:11:56.910 --> 00:11:59.620
and don't forget on the
links in the description.

